WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.000
Say that we’re given JUST this point here.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.490
It’s pretty weird, but if you have a function
that tells you how far away the nearest surface

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.280
is, without telling you anything else, AT
ALL.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.700
That’s actually all that’s needed to build
entire, complex 3d worlds, entirely out of

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:18.700
math.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:24.029
The technique is called ray marching, and
it relies entirely on having a signed distance

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:25.320
function of the scene.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.090
And signed distance functions aren’t too
hard to understand.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.800
If i have a 2 points here, lets just make
some points appear, and we want the distance

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.320
between them, the distance is defined by the
length of p2 minus p1.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.910
So NOWWWW if this were the distance between
say, a point and a circle instead.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:51.670
We can model this with length p2 minus p1
- r, which you might realise gives you positive

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:58.060
values outside the circle, and negative values
inside, hence the signed part of the name.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.860
So lets take our world and visualise it as
a distance field.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:08.010
So for any given point in the world here,
we can use our magic signed distance function

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.020
and that will tell us how far away the nearest
surface is.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.560
Now it doesn't tell us anything about where
it is, but weirdly enough, that doesn't actually

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.630
matter

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.260
To render this distance field, you need to
walk through it.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:25.360
So if we’ve got our eye up here, let’s
say we want to get the pixel colour in this

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.360
direction.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:32.390
So we know from the distance field function
that the closest surface is THIS far away.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:37.040
Now we don't actually know where it is, but
that doesnt matter, we know we can travel

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.000
at least that distance safely along our view
vector.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.021
Then you do the same thing at the new point,
you sample the distance function, figure out

00:01:46.021 --> 00:01:51.950
how far things are, that gives you a safe
distance to travel, and then you can repeat

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.850
that process over and over again, until you’re
close enough to an object.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:03.340
Extending this to 3d is pretty much exactly
the same thing, and that’s where you can

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:09.009
really start to do weird and cool stuff, things
that are super hard to do with normal techniques.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:12.950
Nut before we do that, let's take a step back.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:17.810
The scene ideally looks like this, but in
reality looks more like this.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.689
Because we haven’t got any lighting.

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:25.250
We can assign colour based on depth, which
is basically distance fog, and that’ll give

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.280
us a start, but to light, we need normals.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.140
Now normals are actually quite simple to generate
off of distance fields.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:39.370
At the surface point, you take a couple extra
samples of the distance field near the point,

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:45.150
compute the differences along each axis, and
you’ve got a normal, it's really that simple.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.180
Having normals gives you basic lighting.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:53.489
To get shadows, you simply raymarch towards
any lights, recording how close you got to

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:55.389
any surfaces.

00:02:55.389 --> 00:02:58.650
The awesome thing here being that you get
soft shadows for almost nothing.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.489
So now that we know how to light, let's look
at how scenes are constructed.

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.810
You’ve got your basic spheres like we’ve
seen already, but you’ve also got other

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.029
stuff.

00:03:09.029 --> 00:03:13.260
Some very smart people have already figured
out distance functions for a lot of common

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.980
shapes, cubes, donuts, pyramids, and even
have weird and exotic shapes.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.469
You might be thinking, this is a lot of work
to do really basic stuff.

00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:27.379
And that's sort of true actually.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:32.699
But this is where it starts to get interesting,
because you can combine and distort things

00:03:32.699 --> 00:03:35.839
in ways you just CAN'T with traditional techniques.

00:03:35.839 --> 00:03:41.129
So we have functions for individual shapes,
but what’s not immediately obvious is that

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:46.779
having multiple shapes is as simple as taking
a min of their SDF values.

00:03:46.779 --> 00:03:53.010
So in this case having a box and sphere is
as simple as min of the sphere’s SDF value

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.599
and the box’s SDF value.

00:03:55.599 --> 00:04:02.479
And because combining objects is just a min,
an interesting property of the min of 2 functions

00:04:02.479 --> 00:04:06.599
is that it can be blended together, it can
be smoothed out.

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:11.180
Look at these 2 functions here, I’ve got
just a basic line and a parabola.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.659
Now if i were to take the min of these 2 functions,
it would look something like this, so we’ve

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:17.600
got the min of these 2 functions.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.940
But right where the functions meet, you’ve
got this sharp edge, and ideally we want them

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.400
to smoothly meet instead, kinda blend just
between.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.980
And luckily this is a solved problem already,
smooth maximums exist.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:38.980
So a smooth min is just -smoothmax(-a, -b,
k), for some k smoothing value.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:43.250
So with our 3d objects, I can slowly adjust
the smoothing coefficient here and we got

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:47.360
one just kinda melting into the other like
its’ made of butter or something.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.460
From here it just gets wilder, because now
you can start really messing with these distance

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:52.480
fields.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.940
So combining objects is called a union, which
is one operation in constructive solid geometry,

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.330
or CSG.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:03.700
The opposite of a union being a subtraction,
that’s when you subtract one object from

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:08.810
another, and with sdf’s, this is is a simple
max(-a, b)

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:14.600
The last operation being an intersection,
which is forming an object only where 2 objects

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.140
meet, and again, super easy, that’s a max(a,
b);

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:24.710
Operations to transform objects actually modify
the space itself, so we can take our combined

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:29.230
object, and apply rotations, scales, pretty
similarly to the way you’d expect to do

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:30.840
it for normal 3d objects.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:36.610
But more exotic transformations like bends
and twists are possible as well, which I mean

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:40.980
yeah you could do that in a vertex shader,
so lets see some stuff that isn’t possible

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.020
in a vertex shader.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:47.220
Since you’re actually transforming the space
itself, I can do weirder stuff like repeat

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.920
the space, creating multiple copies of the
same object, and this is pretty much for free

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.230
in the shader.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:58.140
This doesn't stop me from applying any individual
transforms, rotations, or basically do whatever

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.200
I want to each copy either

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:05.660
And we can do this pretty much in any direction,
all the way to infinite, which is pretty neat.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.430
And you may be surprised to know, but pretty
much this entire video was made using shaders,

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:13.040
raymarching, and signed distance functions.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.120
So they’re a pretty versatile and fun thing
to know a bit about.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.830
If you’ve wanted to up your shader game,
check out my GLSL course.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:24.580
I cover a lot of the basic concepts in much
more detail, otherwise, the code will be up

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:25.910
on github.

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:25.980
Until next time, cheers.

